---
/* One rolled-over session, told twice — for "The ledger that lied".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two rails for the same stretch of time: what git recorded, and what the
   run's own ledger wrote. The article's subject is the gap between them — a
   session killed at its ceiling returned before the pass that records commits
   and claims, so the ledger holds a zero that is indistinguishable from a
   measurement. The drawing's job is to make the gap visible as a *shape*: the
   same events on both rails until the kill, then one rail keeps the truth and
   the other writes a zero.

   Same idiom as FigureLoop: panes, hairlines, mono labels, arrows that are
   characters, semantic colour for status only. */
---

<figure class="fig-ledger">
  <div
    class="rails"
    role="img"
    aria-label="The same rolled-over session on two rails. On the git rail: the session works, commits, and is killed at the ceiling — the commits exist. On the ledger rail: the session is killed before its verdict pass runs, so nothing records its commits or claims, and the commit column holds a zero that looks like a measurement."
  >
    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="r-label">what git recorded</p>
      <div class="row">
        <span class="box">works</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">commits <span class="ok">✓</span></span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box kill">killed at the ceiling</span>
      </div>
      <p class="r-note">the work is on the branch — history, in the only place history is kept</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="r-label">what the ledger wrote</p>
      <div class="row">
        <span class="box">works</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box kill">killed at the ceiling</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box gap">verdict pass never runs</span>
      </div>
      <p class="r-note gap-note">
        commits recorded: a zero — written by the return path, not measured from anything
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    A population whose history was never written looks identical to one that
    did nothing. The zero is the dangerous kind of wrong: it has the shape of
    a measurement, and only ground truth — git — can contradict it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig-ledger {
    margin: 0;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-l);
    inline-size: min(100%, var(--measure-wide));
  }

  .rails {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  .rail {
    background: var(--mantle);
    border: var(--rule);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-m) var(--space-s);
  }

  .r-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-m);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .box {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-inline: var(--space-xs);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .arrow {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono);
  }

  .ok {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  /* The kill is status, so it is allowed the colour. */
  .box.kill {
    color: var(--red);
  }

  /* The gap is the subject: the box for the pass that never ran is dashed —
     present in the layout, absent in fact. Muted, not a quiet role: the
     quiet roles sit below the site's own legibility bar for reading text,
     and the border already says "not really here". */
  .box.gap {
    color: var(--muted);
    border-style: dashed;
  }

  .r-note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .gap-note {
    color: var(--red);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
